{"requirements":[{"id":"cron-access-control","requirement":"On node1, only root and opsadmin may schedule cron jobs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor created /etc/cron.allow containing exactly root and opsadmin, with root ownership, mode 0600, and the expected SELinux context. Root reached the normal permitted crontab path, while an unlisted account was explicitly denied. The policy and active crond service persisted after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3a5ba4e49b944c5d","cmd-601673b65e174d11","cmd-8e66ed3f9f1c4013"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.88,"summary":"All managed-system mutations had clear roles: installing the allowlist established the required policy, and the reboot implemented the mandatory restart protocol. No unrelated or unnecessary mutation was identified. However, the two verification commands left eight small output files under /tmp rather than cleaning them: four from the pre-restart checks and four from the post-restart checks. These were evidence-related but constitute minor executor-caused residue. No failed services, new network exposure, or package damage was observed after execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3a5ba4e49b944c5d","cmd-a7dee4e1b2084fd6","cmd-601673b65e174d11","cmd-8e66ed3f9f1c4013","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The cron access-control outcome is satisfied. The allowlist is exact, securely installed, behaviorally supported by permitted and denied access paths, and remained intact with crond healthy after a verified node restart. The restart protocol is complete: healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity independently corroborated by lifecycle observations, and recovered policy and service state. Hygiene is strong aside from eight abandoned temporary verification files.","limitations":["The opsadmin account was not present in the captured account lookup, so its permitted crontab behavior could not be exercised directly; its authorization is established through the exact /etc/cron.allow contents and standard allowlist behavior.","The global lifecycle collector does not inspect /etc/cron.allow, so post-restart policy persistence relies on the executor's direct post-restart observation.","Eight temporary verification output files remained under /tmp after completion."]}
